  --destructive-foreground: #ffffff;
  --border: #e2e3e6;
  --input: #e2e3e6;
  --ring: oklch(0.62 0.19 152 / 40%);
  --success: oklch(0.6 0.17 152);
  --warning: oklch(0.7 0.15 70);
  --danger: oklch(0.58 0.22 25);
  --hairline: oklch(0 0 0 / 8%);
  --glass: oklch(1 0 0 / 70%);
  --glow-success: 0 0 0 1px oklch(0.62 0.19 152 / 30%), 0 8px 40px -8px oklch(0.62 0.19 152 / 40%);
  --glow-warning: 0 0 24px -6px oklch(0.7 0.15 70 / 50%);
  --gradient-ambient: radial-gradient(...);
  --panel-... 
  --surface: oklch(0 0 0 / 3%);
  --nav-bg: ...
}
```

Also need `--panel` gradient variables. I'll refactor `panel` utility to use `--panel-top`/`--panel-bottom` and shadow vars, defined in `:root` and overridden in `.light`. Or simpler: add override selector `html.light .panel { ... }`. But `@utility panel` compiles to a class; overriding with a plain rule `html.light .panel { background: ... }` should work since specificity is higher. Let me do that to minimize refactor.

Similarly `html.light .glass`? glass uses `var(--glass)` and `var(--hairline)` which I override, so fine.

Mobile nav: `bg-[#0b0b0d]` → replace with a token `bg-[var(--nav-bg)]` and define `--nav-bg` in root and light. Actually simpler: create a utility class or use inline style. I'll add `--nav-bg` var and change class to `bg-[var(--nav-bg)]`. Tailwind arbitrary value works.

Surface overlays: add `--surface` var; replace `bg-white/[0.025]` → `bg-[var(--surface)]`, `bg-white/[0.04]` → `bg-[var(--surface-strong)]`, `bg-white/[0.06]` → `bg-[var(--surface-hover)]`. Define:
- dark: --surface: oklch(1 0 0 / 2.5%); --surface-strong: oklch(1 0 0/4%); --surface-hover: oklch(1 0 0/6%)
- light: --surface: oklch(0 0 0/3%); --surface-strong: oklch(0 0 0/5%); --surface-hover: oklch(0 0 0/7%)

Wait but these var names as Tailwind color tokens: `bg-[var(--surface)]` works with arbitrary. Fine.

Also `text-primary` in light should be darker (I set primary darker). Good. But note many components use `text-success` for numbers; on light bg oklch(0.84 ...) bright green is low contrast. I'll darken success in light.

Also there might be `glow-success` shadow using a bright green — fine.

Also the fonts and layout fine.

Also `Toaster theme="dark"` hardcoded — should follow. I'll make it dynamic? The Toaster is in server layout. Could set to "system" so it follows OS, but our theme is explicit. Simplest: leave as is, or make a client ThemedToaster. Minor. I'll leave Toaster but maybe set theme to "system"? Not tied to our class. I'll leave it; not critical. Actually to be clean, I could create a client component that reads the theme. But keep scope tight—leave Toaster dark? In light mode toasts dark is okay-ish. Hmm, user asked only for toggle. I'll leave toaster.

Actually, let me make Toaster theme follow via a small client wrapper to be polished. Eh, keep scope minimal but functional. I'll leave it.

Now the ThemeToggle component:
```
"use client";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { Moon, Sun } from "lucide-react";

const STORAGE_KEY = "swapdesk-theme";

export function ThemeToggle({ expanded }: { expanded: boolean }) {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState<"dark" | "light">("dark");

  useEffect(() => {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    const initial = stored === "light" ? "light" : "dark";
    setTheme(initial);
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle("light", initial === "light");
  }, []);

  const toggle = () => {
    const next = theme === "light" ? "dark" : "light";
    setTheme(next);
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle("light", next === "light");
    localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, next);
  };
  ...
}
```

But eslint rule `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` may flag the setTheme in the mount effect. I'll add eslint-disable comment. In AppShell the BackendStatus effect calls setState inside async callback, allowed. Here setTheme is direct in effect → flagged. Add disable comment.

Actually simpler: initialize state lazily from a function that reads document? Can't at SSR (window undefined). Use useState(() => "dark") then effect. Add disable comment. Or avoid state entirely: read class from DOM on render? React won't re-render on class change from toggle unless state. So keep state + disable comment.

Alternatively initialize from localStorage in a way that matches the pre-hydration script: The script sets the class before React. Then in component, we can derive initial by reading documentElement class inside useState initializer with typeof window check... but SSR/hydration mismatch risk. Using effect with disable is standard.

Let me also add the blocking script in layout head to prevent flash:
```
<script dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: `(function(){try{var t=localStorage.getItem('swapdesk-theme');if(t==='light'){document.documentElement.classList.add('light')}}catch(e){}})()` }} />
```
Place in <head>? Next App Router: can put <script> in the html or in body. Better use next/script with strategy="beforeInteractive" inside html? beforeInteractive scripts must be in <head>? Simpler: put the inline script as the first child of <body> or in <html>. Actually placing inside `<head>` requires a `<head>` tag; Next allows `<head>` in layout? In App Router you can add a `<head>`? Actually you can't add `<head>` easily; but you can put `<script>` directly in the returned JSX inside `<html>` before `<body>`? Next hoists? Let me just put the inline script as the first element inside `<body>` — it executes before content paint enough to avoid flash (body not painted yet). Actually script in body runs before subsequent DOM is painted typically. Good enough.

Hmm, but class on <html> must be added before CSS paints. A script at top of body runs after CSS loaded but before body content paints; the html background might flash. Since our default is dark and light users get a brief dark flash. Acceptable but not perfect.

Better: use `next/script` with `strategy="beforeInteractive"` which Next injects into head. Let me use that: import Script from "next/script" and place `<Script id="theme-init" strategy="beforeInteractive" dangerouslySetInnerHTML=... />` inside the html. Next docs: beforeInteractive scripts are injected into head. Good.

Also update viewport colorScheme to "light dark" so browser UI adapts.

Now the toggle button placement: sidebar footer currently:
```
<div className="mt-auto flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
  <BackendStatus expanded={expanded} />
  <button ...collapse...>
</div>
```
Add ThemeToggle above BackendStatus or between. I'll add `<ThemeToggle expanded={expanded} />` first.

Let me design ThemeToggle rendering consistent with BackendStatus style:
```
<button
  type="button"
  onClick={toggle}
  aria-label={theme === "light" ? "Switch to dark mode" : "Switch to light mode"}
  title={...}
  className={`grid h-10 place-items-center rounded-xl border border-hairline text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-accent/60 hover:text-foreground ${expanded ? "w-full grid-cols-none px-3 gap-2 grid-flow-col" : "w-10"}`}
>
  <span className="flex items-center gap-2">
    {theme === "light" ? <Moon .../> : <Sun .../>}
    {expanded && <span className="text-[11px]">{theme === "light" ? "Dark mode" : "Light mode"}</span>}
  </span>
</button>
```

Let me match BackendStatus class approach (grid h-10 place-items-center w-10, expanded w-full). I'll keep simple.

Now implement. Order:
1. globals.css edits: add surface/nav vars to :root, add html.light block with all overrides + panel override.
2. AppShell: replace hardcoded bg-#0b0b0d and bg-white/[0.06], add ThemeToggle import + render.
